// Field Guide cards first taught in the A camp (The Summit). See CONTENT-GUIDE.md.
// The capstone: plan, build, test, audit, ship and present one real app (the Summit Log).
// Platform-specific details live ONLY in `os` fields.

export const GUIDE_CAMPA = {
  // ─────────────── Planning the climb ───────────────
  'project-planning': {
    term: 'Project planning', short: 'Deciding what you are building, for whom, and in what order, before you write the code.',
    what: 'Project planning is the short, written thinking you do before building an app: the **problem** it solves, **who** it is for, **what** it must do, what it will **not** do (yet), the **pieces** it needs (data, API, screen), and the **order** you will build them in. It works like the route plan a climbing team agrees on at base camp: everyone knows the goal before the climb starts.\n\nIn Camp 6 you planned a single command-line tool. A whole app has more moving parts, so the plan grows a little: a one-page **project brief**, a list of **requirements**, an **MVP** (the smallest useful version), and a **build plan** (the order of slices).',
    how: 'A one-page project brief has six parts. Write it in plain words, in a README or a notes file, before any code:\n1. **Problem**: one or two sentences. "I forget which peaks I climbed, how high they were, and whether I made the top."\n2. **User**: who has the problem. "A hiker or climber who logs trips on their phone."\n3. **Goal**: what success looks like. "Log a climb in under 30 seconds and see my summit stats."\n4. **Scope**: what is IN the first version and, just as important, what is OUT. "In: log, list, filter, delete, stats. Out: photos, friends, maps."\n5. **Pieces**: the parts you need. "A database table for climbs, an API with five routes, one React screen, tests."\n6. **Risks and unknowns**: what could go wrong or what you have never done. "Never deployed a database before. Map features could cost money."\n\nThen turn the brief into requirements (next card), cut them down to an MVP, and order the work into a build plan. Revisit the plan whenever you learn something: a plan is a living document that you update as you learn.',
    usage: [
      { code: '# Summit Log: project brief\n\nProblem: I forget which peaks I climbed and whether I summited.\nUser:    a climber logging trips on a phone.\nGoal:    log a climb in under 30 seconds; see summit stats.\nIN:      log, list, filter by summited, delete, stats.\nOUT:     photos, friends, maps, sign-up (later, maybe).\nPieces:  climbs table · Express API (5 routes) · React screen · tests.\nRisks:   first deploy; map APIs can bill per request.', note: 'A whole brief on one screen. This sits at the top of your README.' },
      { code: '// TODO (in order)\n// 1. climbs table + sample rows\n// 2. GET /api/climbs\n// 3. POST /api/climbs with validation\n// 4. list screen\n// 5. add form\n// 6. tests, audit, deploy', note: 'The brief becomes an ordered checklist you tick off.' },
    ],
    uses: ['Every app, side project or portfolio piece you build from now on.', 'Pitching an idea to a team, a client or an interviewer: the brief IS the pitch.', 'Estimating: once the pieces are listed, you can guess how long each takes.', 'Saying no to feature creep: the OUT list is your shield.'],
    when: ['Before the first line of code of anything bigger than a single script.', 'When you are stuck or lost mid-build: reread the brief and ask "which piece am I on?"', 'When someone asks "can it also do X?": check the scope list first.'],
    whenNot: ['Don\'t plan for weeks. A one-page brief and a short requirement list are enough for a solo project; the rest you learn by building.', 'Don\'t plan features you have no way to build yet. Put them in OUT and move on.', 'Don\'t treat the plan as frozen. When a requirement turns out wrong, update the plan, then the code.'],
    mistakes: [
      { prose: 'Starting with the fun part (colours, logo, animations) before the app can save a single record.', why: 'Looks great, does nothing. Build the parts that make it work first; polish comes last.' },
      { prose: 'No OUT list, so every good idea gets added "while I\'m at it."', why: 'That is scope creep: the project never finishes. Writing down what is OUT keeps the project finishable.' },
      { prose: 'Keeping the plan only in your head.', why: 'You forget it within a week, and nobody else can help. Write it down where the code lives (the README).' },
    ],
    tips: ['If you can\'t say the problem in two sentences, you don\'t understand it yet. Keep rewriting until you can.', 'Name the risks early. The scary unknown (first deploy, first database) should be tried in a tiny test, not discovered on launch day.', 'Keep the brief at the top of the README. It becomes the first thing visitors and future-you read.'],
    related: ['requirements', 'mvp', 'build-plan', 'midterm-planning', 'presenting-work'],
  },
  requirements: {
    term: 'Requirements (user stories + done criteria)', short: 'Plain-language statements of what the app must do, each with a test you can check.',
    what: 'A requirement says **what** the app must do, not **how** the code does it. The most common format is the **user story**:\n\n"As a **who**, I want to **what**, so that **why**."\n\nEach story gets **acceptance criteria** (also called "done when"): concrete, checkable facts that prove the story works. "As a climber, I want to delete a climb I logged by mistake, so that my log stays accurate. Done when: DELETE /api/climbs/:id returns 204 and the climb no longer appears in the list."\n\nThere are also **non-functional requirements**: qualities rather than features. "Pages load in under 2 seconds on a phone." "Passwords are never stored in plain text." "Works with a keyboard and a screen reader."',
    how: 'How to write good requirements:\n1. **One story, one need.** Not "log, edit, share and print climbs."\n2. **Use the user\'s words**, not tech words. "See only climbs I summited", not "filter query param".\n3. **Keep the why.** It tells you what matters when you have to cut something.\n4. **Make "done" testable.** If you can\'t imagine a check that passes or fails, it isn\'t a requirement yet. "Fast" is a wish; "under 2 seconds" is a requirement.\n5. **Include the sad paths.** What happens with a missing field, a wrong id, no internet? "Done when: a climb with no peak name is rejected with a 400 and a clear error."\n6. **Number them** (R1, R2…) so tests, commits and the README can point back to them.\n\nThe "done when" lines become your test list and your API contract. That is why they are worth the ten minutes.',
    usage: [
      { code: 'R1  As a climber, I want to log a climb (peak, height, date, summited?),\n    so that I never forget a trip.\n    Done when: POST /api/climbs with a valid climb returns 201 and the saved climb.\n    Done when: a climb with no peak returns 400 with "peak is required".', note: 'One story, a happy-path check and a sad-path check.' },
      { code: 'R2  As a climber, I want to see only the climbs I summited,\n    so that I can track my progress.\n    Done when: GET /api/climbs?summited=true returns only summited climbs.', note: 'The done line is already half of a test and half of an API design.' },
      { code: 'N1  Every screen works with a keyboard alone.\nN2  No secret keys appear in front-end code.\nN3  The app costs nothing to run for up to 100 users.', note: 'Non-functional requirements: qualities, still checkable.' },
      { code: 'const requirements = [\n  { who: "climber", want: "log a climb", why: "I never forget a trip",\n    done: "POST /api/climbs returns 201" },\n]\nconst story = r => `As a ${r.who}, I want to ${r.want}, so that ${r.why}.`\nconsole.log(story(requirements[0]))', out: 'As a climber, I want to log a climb, so that I never forget a trip.', note: 'Requirements are just data too. The try-it builds exactly this.' },
    ],
    uses: ['Deciding what to build and checking you built it.', 'Writing tests: each "done when" line becomes one or more tests.', 'Talking with non-developers (clients, teammates) in words both sides understand.', 'Job interviews and take-home projects: listing requirements first shows you think before you type.'],
    when: ['After the project brief, before designing the data or the API.', 'Whenever a feature is unclear: write the story and its done line, then build.', 'When a bug report comes in: turn it into a requirement with a done line, then fix it.'],
    whenNot: ['Don\'t write requirements in code terms ("add a useState for filter"). That is the how; requirements are the what.', 'Don\'t write vague ones ("user friendly", "fast", "secure") without a measurable done line.', 'Don\'t write fifty stories for a first version. Write them, then cut most of them into "later" (that is the MVP step).'],
    mistakes: [
      { prose: '"As a user, I want the app to be good."', why: 'Untestable. Nobody can say when it is done. Name the user, one need, and a check that passes or fails.' },
      { prose: 'Only happy paths: every request is valid, every network call works.', why: 'Real users send blanks, typos and huge numbers. Add sad-path done lines, or the code won\'t handle them either.' },
      { prose: 'Requirements that dictate the code: "Use a for loop to filter climbs."', why: 'That locks in a solution too early. Say what must happen ("show only summited climbs"); choose the how while building.' },
    ],
    tips: ['If a requirement has "and" in the middle, it is probably two requirements.', 'Write the "done when" line as if it were a test name. Later, it literally will be.', 'Keep the list in the README so anyone can see what the app promises.'],
    related: ['project-planning', 'mvp', 'final-testing', 'api-contract'],
  },
  mvp: {
    term: 'MVP (minimum viable product)', stands: 'Minimum Viable Product', short: 'The smallest version of your app that still solves the real problem for a real user.',
    what: 'An MVP is the **smallest** set of features that makes the app genuinely **useful**. Not a broken demo, not a half-built everything: a complete, working, small thing.\n\nClimbers call this travelling light: every extra kilo slows the whole team, so they carry only what the summit needs. For the Summit Log, "log a climb, list my climbs, delete a mistake" is useful on day one. Photos, friends and maps can come later, if users actually want them.\n\nThe classic picture: to get someone across town, don\'t build one wheel, then an axle, then a car body (useless until the very end). Build a skateboard, then a scooter, then a bike, then a car. Each step is small, but every step gets the person somewhere.',
    how: 'How to cut a feature list down to an MVP:\n1. **List every feature** you can think of. Get them out of your head.\n2. **Tag each one** with a priority. A common scheme is **MoSCoW**: **Must** have (the app is pointless without it), **Should** have (important, not vital), **Could** have (nice extra), **Won\'t** have this time.\n3. **The MVP is the Musts**, and only the Musts. Be ruthless: if the app still solves the problem without it, it is not a Must.\n4. **Estimate** each Must in hours. If the total is scary, cut again, or split big features into a smaller first version ("delete" before "undo delete").\n5. **Ship the MVP**, show it to real people, and let their reactions decide what comes next.\n\n"Viable" matters as much as "minimum": the Musts must work properly, including validation, errors and a basic, accessible screen. Small is fine; shoddy is not.',
    usage: [
      { code: 'Log a climb            MUST    6 h\nList my climbs         MUST    4 h\nDelete a climb         MUST    2 h\nSummit stats           SHOULD  3 h\nPhoto uploads          COULD  12 h\nFriends and comments   COULD  20 h', note: 'Six ideas; three Musts. MVP = 12 hours instead of 47.' },
      { code: 'const features = [\n  { name: "Log a climb", priority: "must", hours: 6 },\n  { name: "Photo uploads", priority: "could", hours: 12 },\n]\nconst mvp = list => list.filter(f => f.priority === "must")\nconsole.log(mvp(features).map(f => f.name))', out: "[ 'Log a climb' ]", note: 'The MVP cut is literally a filter.' },
    ],
    uses: ['Finishing projects at all: an MVP gives you a real finish line.', 'Startups and product teams testing whether an idea is worth more work.', 'Portfolio pieces: a small app that fully works beats a big one that half works.', 'Hackathons and deadlines: decide the Musts in the first hour.'],
    when: ['Right after writing requirements, before designing anything.', 'Whenever the project feels too big to finish: cut back to the Musts.', 'When deciding what to build next after launch: what are users actually asking for?'],
    whenNot: ['Don\'t use "MVP" as an excuse to skip quality: no validation, no error handling or no accessibility is not "minimum", it is broken.', 'Don\'t cut security on things that handle personal data or money. Protecting users is always a Must.', 'Don\'t keep adding "just one more" Must. If everything is a Must, nothing is.'],
    mistakes: [
      { prose: 'Calling a login screen with nothing behind it an MVP.', why: 'It isn\'t viable: no user problem is solved. The MVP must do the core job end to end, even if only in a tiny way.' },
      { prose: 'Building every feature halfway instead of a few features fully.', why: 'Five half-features are zero working features. Finish the Musts completely before touching a Should.' },
      { prose: 'Polishing for months before showing anyone.', why: 'You might be building the wrong thing. Show the MVP early; real reactions tell you what to build next.' },
    ],
    tips: ['Ask of every feature: "If this was missing, would the app still solve the problem?" If yes, it waits.', 'Write the cut features in a "Later" list so you don\'t lose them. Saying "later" is easier than saying "no".', 'A good MVP usually feels a little embarrassing to its builder. That is the right size.'],
    related: ['requirements', 'build-plan', 'project-planning'],
  },
  'build-plan': {
    term: 'Build plan (vertical slices)', short: 'The order you build the pieces in: thin end-to-end slices, each one working before the next starts.',
    what: 'A build plan turns the MVP into an ordered list of small, finishable steps. The best plans use **vertical slices**: each step goes through every layer (data → API → screen) for one small feature, so after each step something real works end to end.\n\nThe opposite is building in **horizontal layers**: the whole database, then the whole API, then the whole UI. Nothing works until the very end, and problems where the layers meet are found last, when they are most expensive.\n\nThink of fixing ropes up a face: you anchor one section solidly, then climb it, then fix the next. You never string 500 metres of rope and only then check the first anchor.',
    how: 'How to write a build plan:\n1. **Slice 0, the walking skeleton**: the thinnest possible end-to-end path. A server with `GET /api/health` returning `{ ok: true }`, a page that shows "API ok". It proves every layer can talk to the next.\n2. **One slice per Must**, smallest and most important first. Each slice lists its data change, its route(s), its screen change and its test. "Slice 1: list climbs. Table + seed rows → GET /api/climbs → list screen → test."\n3. **Order by dependency**: you can\'t delete a climb before you can list one.\n4. **Define done for each slice**: it runs, its test passes, it is committed. Then, and only then, start the next one.\n5. **Commit after every slice** with a message naming it, so you always have a safe point to return to.\n6. **Leave buffer.** Things take longer than estimates. Plan for it rather than being surprised.\n\nThe data model usually comes first inside each slice: the shape of the stored data decides the shape of the API, which decides the screen.',
    usage: [
      { code: 'Slice 0  walking skeleton: GET /api/health → page shows "API ok"\nSlice 1  list:   climbs table + 2 seed rows → GET /api/climbs → <ul> of climbs\nSlice 2  log:    POST /api/climbs + validation → form → appears in list\nSlice 3  delete: DELETE /api/climbs/:id → delete button\nSlice 4  stats:  GET /api/stats → "2 climbs, 1 summited"\nSlice 5  tests, self-audit, deploy, README', note: 'Each line works end to end before the next begins.' },
      { code: '$ git commit -m "Slice 1: list climbs (table, GET /api/climbs, list screen)"', note: 'A commit per slice gives you a safe point to return to.' },
    ],
    uses: ['Turning a scary project into a list of evenings you can actually finish.', 'Spotting integration problems on day one, not on launch day.', 'Showing progress to anyone at any time: there is always a working version.', 'Team work: slices can be shared out, because each one is self-contained.'],
    when: ['Right after you have cut the MVP.', 'Whenever you are unsure what to do next: open the plan, take the next slice.', 'After a surprise (an API works differently than expected): re-order the remaining slices.'],
    whenNot: ['Don\'t build a whole layer before anything works end to end ("I\'ll do the entire database first").', 'Don\'t start a new slice while the current one is broken. Unfinished slices pile up and hide bugs.', 'Don\'t make slices so big they take a week. If a slice can\'t be finished in one or two sessions, split it.'],
    mistakes: [
      { prose: 'Writing all the routes, then all the screens, then trying them together for the first time.', why: 'Every mismatch (a field called heightM in one place and height in another) shows up at once, at the end. Slices find each mismatch while it is small.' },
      { prose: 'No commits until "it\'s all finished".', why: 'One bad change and there is no safe point to go back to. Commit when each slice works.' },
      { prose: 'Leaving tests and deployment as "the last slice, if there\'s time".', why: 'There is never time. Write each slice\'s test as part of that slice, and deploy the walking skeleton early so deploy problems surface early.' },
    ],
    tips: ['Deploy slice 0. A live "API ok" page in week one removes the biggest unknown.', 'Keep the plan as a checklist in the README and tick slices off as you commit them.', 'If you finish a slice early, don\'t start a Could. Take the next Must.'],
    related: ['mvp', 'integration', 'final-testing', 'commit'],
  },

  // ─────────────── Wiring the pieces together ───────────────
  'api-contract': {
    term: 'API contract', short: 'The written agreement between front end and back end: routes, request shapes, response shapes and errors.',
    what: 'An API contract is a precise description of every route your back end offers: the **method and path**, what the **request** must contain, what the **response** looks like (status code and JSON shape) for success, and what it looks like for each **error**. The front end and the tests are written against the contract, and the back end promises to honour it.\n\nBoth sides must follow the same contract. If the server sends `heightM` and the screen reads `height`, the screen shows nothing and no error appears anywhere.',
    how: 'Write the contract before the code, in the README or a notes file. For each route:\n• **Method + path**: `GET /api/climbs/:id`\n• **Input**: path params (`:id` is a number), query string (`?summited=true`), body fields with types and rules (`peak`: non-empty string)\n• **Success**: status and exact JSON shape: `200 { id, peak, heightM, summited, date }`\n• **Errors**: one line per case: `400 { error: "id must be a number" }`, `404 { error: "Climb not found" }`\n\nGood contract habits:\n1. **Consistent shapes.** Lists wrapped in an object (`{ climbs: [...] }`) so you can add fields later; errors always `{ error: "…" }` (or `{ errors: [...] }` for validation lists).\n2. **Correct status codes.** 200 OK, 201 Created, 204 No Content, 400 bad input, 401 not allowed, 404 not found, 500 the server broke.\n3. **Remember the query string and params arrive as strings.** `req.params.id` is `"2"`, not `2`; `req.query.summited` is `"true"`, not `true`. Convert them on purpose.\n4. **Never trust the client for things the server owns**: the server picks the `id`, not the request body.\n5. **Change the contract on purpose**: update the doc, the server, the front end and the tests together.',
    usage: [
      { code: 'GET    /api/climbs              200 { climbs: [Climb] }\nGET    /api/climbs?summited=true 200 { climbs: [Climb] }  (only summited)\nGET    /api/climbs/:id          200 Climb | 400 { error } | 404 { error }\nPOST   /api/climbs   body Climb  201 Climb | 400 { errors: [...] } | 401 { error }\nDELETE /api/climbs/:id          204 | 401 { error } | 404 { error }\n\nClimb = { id: number, peak: string, heightM: number,\n          summited: boolean, date: "YYYY-MM-DD" }', note: 'The whole Summit Log contract on one screen.' },
      { code: 'app.get("/api/climbs/:id", (req, res) => {\n  const id = Number(req.params.id)\n  if (Number.isNaN(id)) return res.status(400).json({ error: "id must be a number" })\n  const climb = climbs.find(c => c.id === id)\n  if (!climb) return res.status(404).json({ error: "Climb not found" })\n  res.json(climb)\n})', out: 'GET /api/climbs/2   → 200 { id: 2, peak: "Mera Peak", … }\nGET /api/climbs/99  → 404 { error: "Climb not found" }\nGET /api/climbs/abc → 400 { error: "id must be a number" }', note: 'One route honouring all three lines of its contract.' },
    ],
    uses: ['Letting the front end and back end be built separately (by you on different days, or by different people) and still fit.', 'Writing tests: every contract line is a test case.', 'Documentation for anyone who wants to use your API.', 'Catching breaking changes: if a change breaks a contract line, the front end will break too.'],
    when: ['Before writing the server routes or the screen that calls them.', 'Every time you add or change a route.', 'When the front end "shows nothing": compare what the server actually sends with the contract.'],
    whenNot: ['Don\'t let the contract drift: changing the server without updating the doc and the front end is how silent bugs are born.', 'Don\'t design a contract that leaks internals, like returning every database column (password hashes, emails) just because it was easy.', 'Don\'t over-design for a tiny MVP. Five well-defined routes beat twenty speculative ones.'],
    mistakes: [
      { wrong: 'climbs.find(c => c.id === req.params.id)', result: 'undefined (so every lookup returns 404)', why: 'req.params.id is the string "2", and strict equality says 2 !== "2". Convert with Number() first.' },
      { wrong: 'if (req.query.summited) { … only summited … }', result: '?summited=false ALSO filters to summited', why: 'The query value is the string "false", which is truthy. Compare the text: req.query.summited === "true".' },
      { wrong: 'res.json({ id: req.body.id, ...req.body })', result: 'Clients choose their own ids and can overwrite other records or add any field they like', why: 'The server owns the id. Build the saved object from known fields only and assign the id yourself.' },
    ],
    tips: ['Paste a real example request and response under every route in the doc. Examples catch ambiguity that descriptions miss.', 'Pick one naming style (camelCase like heightM) for JSON and stick to it everywhere.', 'When a screen shows nothing, log the raw response first. Most of the time the shape isn\'t what you assumed.'],
    related: ['requirements', 'integration', 'status-codes-basics', 'json-api', 'final-testing'],
  },
  integration: {
    term: 'Integration (wiring the layers together)', short: 'Making the separate parts (database, API, front end) work together as one app, end to end.',
    what: 'Integration is the work of connecting pieces that each work alone, so they work **together**: the screen calls the API, the API reads and writes the database, and the answer flows back to the screen in the shape everyone expects.\n\nMost bugs in a real app live at the **seams** between layers, not inside them: a field named differently on each side, a number arriving as a string, a request sent before the data loaded, an error the screen never shows. Integration is where you find and fix those.\n\nThe full trip of one action in the Summit Log: tap **Log climb** → React handler runs → `fetch` sends `POST /api/climbs` with JSON → Express route checks and validates → a parameterized `INSERT` saves it → the server replies `201` with the saved climb → the front end adds it to state → React re-renders the list.',
    how: 'How to integrate without pain:\n1. **Integrate early**, with a walking skeleton (one route, one screen), and keep it working.\n2. **One seam at a time.** Get the API returning the right JSON (check it in the server log or with test requests) before wiring the screen to it.\n3. **Isolate the calls** in one small module (an `api` object with `listClimbs()`, `addClimb()`), so the components don\'t care whether data comes from a fake list, the real server, or a test.\n4. **Handle all three states** of every request in the UI: **loading**, **success**, **error**. The happy path is only one of three.\n5. **Match the contract exactly**: same field names, same types, same status codes. When in doubt, log what actually arrived.\n6. **Test across the seam**: an **integration test** sends a real request through the route and checks the response, rather than testing one function alone.\n\nIn React, data from an API usually loads in `useEffect` and lands in state; the list renders from that state, so the screen updates by itself when the data arrives.',
    usage: [
      { code: 'const api = {\n  listClimbs: () => fetch("/api/climbs").then(r => r.json()),\n}\n\nfunction App() {\n  const [climbs, setClimbs] = useState(null)\n  useEffect(() => {\n    api.listClimbs().then(data => setClimbs(data.climbs))\n  }, [])\n  if (climbs === null) return <p>Loading...</p>\n  return <ul>{climbs.map(c => <li key={c.id}>{c.peak}</li>)}</ul>\n}', note: 'The screen asks the API once, shows "Loading..." until the answer arrives, then renders the list from state.' },
      { code: 'async function addClimb(climb) {\n  const res = await fetch("/api/climbs", {\n    method: "POST",\n    headers: { "Content-Type": "application/json" },\n    body: JSON.stringify(climb),\n  })\n  const data = await res.json()\n  if (!res.ok) throw new Error(data.errors ? data.errors.join(", ") : data.error)\n  return data\n}', note: 'Sending JSON across the seam, and turning a 400 into an error the screen can show.' },
    ],
    uses: ['Every full-stack app: front end, back end and database have to meet somewhere.', 'Swapping a fake data source for the real API without touching your components.', 'Connecting to third-party services (payments, maps, email) through their APIs.', 'Debugging "it works on its own but not together" problems.'],
    when: ['From slice 0 onward: wire the thinnest path first and keep it working.', 'Every time a new route or screen is added.', 'Whenever the screen shows nothing or the wrong thing: walk the request from click to database and back.'],
    whenNot: ['Don\'t wait until every layer is "finished" to connect them. Late integration is where projects stall.', 'Don\'t scatter `fetch` calls across every component. Keep them in one place, so a contract change is a one-file fix.', 'Don\'t assume a request succeeded. Check `res.ok`, and give the user a visible error when it didn\'t.'],
    mistakes: [
      { wrong: 'setClimbs(data)   (the API sends { climbs: [...] })', result: 'TypeError: climbs.map is not a function', why: 'You stored the wrapper object, not the array inside it. The contract says the list lives at data.climbs.' },
      { wrong: 'useEffect(() => { api.listClimbs().then(d => setClimbs(d.climbs)) })', result: 'The API is called again after every render, forever', why: 'With no dependency array the effect runs after every render, and each setClimbs causes another render. Add [] to run it once.' },
      { prose: 'Only coding the success case: no loading text, no error message.', why: 'On a slow phone connection the screen sits blank, and when the server says 400 the user never learns why. Handle loading, success and error.' },
    ],
    tips: ['When something is wrong between layers, check both ends: what the browser sent (devtools Network tab) and what the server received (its log).', 'Wrap all API calls in one small module. It is the best way to keep the connection between layers tidy.', 'Fake the API first (a function returning a Promise of sample data) so you can build the screen before the server exists.'],
    related: ['api-contract', 'build-plan', 'fetch', 'async-await', 'final-testing'],
  },

  // ─────────────── Proving it and checking it ───────────────
  'final-testing': {
    term: 'Final testing (checking the whole app)', short: 'Proving the whole app meets its requirements: automated tests plus a hands-on check of every user path.',
    what: 'Final testing is the last check before you call the app done or put it online. It answers one question: **does the app do everything its requirements promised, including when things go wrong?**\n\nIt has two halves:\n• **Automated tests**: code that checks your code. **Unit tests** check one function (does `summitRate([])` return 0?). **Integration tests** send a request through a route and check the response (does `POST /api/climbs` with no peak return 400?). They run in seconds, every time you change anything.\n• **A manual test pass**: you, using the real app like a user would, following a written checklist built from the requirements: on a phone-sized screen, with the keyboard only, with bad input, with the server stopped.\n\nTests don\'t stop you making mistakes, but they catch them before your users do.',
    how: 'Build the final test list from your requirements:\n1. **One test (or more) per "done when" line.** R1 "valid climb returns 201" → a test. R1 "no peak returns 400" → another test.\n2. **Test the edges**, the places bugs hide: empty lists, zero, the biggest allowed value and one past it, missing fields, wrong types, unknown ids, duplicate entries.\n3. **Arrange, act, assert**: set up the data, run the thing, check the result with `expect(...)`. One behaviour per test, with a name that reads like a requirement: "summitRate: no climbs is 0".\n4. **Watch a test fail first.** A test that has never failed might not be testing anything. Break the code on purpose, see red, fix, see green.\n5. **Fix the code, not the test**, when a correct test fails. The failing test just found a bug for free.\n6. **Run the manual checklist**: every user story by hand; keyboard only; a narrow phone screen; offline or server down; a hard refresh mid-task.\n7. **Run everything again after the last change.** The final fix is the one most likely to break something else.\n\nIn this game, `test("name", fn)` and `expect(x).toBe(y)` print `✓ name` or `✗ name: reason`. Real projects use the same pattern with Node\'s built-in test runner (`node --test`) or a test package installed with npm.',
    usage: [
      { code: 'function summitRate(climbs) {\n  if (climbs.length === 0) return 0\n  const up = climbs.filter(c => c.summited).length\n  return Math.round(up / climbs.length * 100)\n}\n\ntest("summitRate: 1 of 2 summited is 50", () => {\n  expect(summitRate([{ summited: true }, { summited: false }])).toBe(50)\n})\ntest("summitRate: no climbs is 0", () => {\n  expect(summitRate([])).toBe(0)\n})', out: '✓ summitRate: 1 of 2 summited is 50\n✓ summitRate: no climbs is 0', note: 'Without the `length === 0` line, the second test prints ✗ (0 / 0 is NaN). The test found a real bug.' },
      { code: 'Manual pass, Summit Log v1\n[ ] Log a climb on a phone-sized screen\n[ ] Log with an empty peak: clear error shown, nothing saved\n[ ] Delete a climb; refresh; it is still gone\n[ ] Tab through the whole page; every button reachable and visible\n[ ] Stop the server: the screen shows an error, not a blank page', note: 'The human half: built straight from the requirements.' },
    ],
    uses: ['Knowing, not hoping, that the app works before strangers use it.', 'Changing code later without fear: if the tests stay green, you didn\'t break the promises.', 'Showing employers and collaborators that your work is reliable: a test suite is proof.', 'Catching edge-case bugs (empty lists, NaN, duplicates) before users do.'],
    when: ['Before every deploy, and after the last change before calling anything done.', 'Each slice\'s tests as you build it; the full list at the end.', 'Whenever a bug is found: first write a test that fails because of it, then fix it.'],
    whenNot: ['Don\'t test only the happy path. Most real bugs live in empty, missing, wrong or huge inputs.', 'Don\'t change a correct test to make it pass. Fix the code it caught.', 'Don\'t rely on tests alone for screens. Automated checks can\'t tell you a button is confusing or unreadable; a manual pass can.'],
    mistakes: [
      { prose: 'Writing tests after the code and copying whatever the code currently returns into expect().', why: 'Then the tests just lock in today\'s bugs. Expected values must come from the requirements, not from the output.' },
      { wrong: 'expect(summitRate([])).toBe(NaN)', result: '✓ passes, and the bug ships', why: 'The test agreed with the bug. "No climbs" should be 0%, per the requirement. The test must say what is RIGHT.' },
      { prose: 'One giant test that checks ten things.', why: 'When it fails you don\'t know which promise broke. One behaviour per test, named like a requirement.' },
    ],
    tips: ['Name tests like sentences: "POST with no peak returns 400". The list of names becomes a readable spec.', 'Every bug you fix earns a test. That bug can never come back silently.', 'Keep the manual checklist in the repo. You will run it before every release, and so will anyone who helps you.'],
    related: ['requirements', 'edge-cases', 'self-audit', 'debugging-process'],
  },
  'self-audit': {
    term: 'Self-audit (judging your own app)', short: 'Inspecting your own project for security, privacy, cost and reliability problems before anyone else finds them.',
    what: 'A self-audit means checking your own work the way you would check someone else\'s. You have learned to check other people\'s claims and tools; now you check your own app the same way, before it goes public.\n\nIt is not about whether the app works (that is testing). It asks whether the app is **safe, private, affordable and resilient**:\n• **Secrets**: is any key, password or token in the code, the front end, the repo or the logs?\n• **Validation**: does the server check every input, or does it trust whatever arrives?\n• **Privacy**: what personal data do you collect? Do you need it? Who can see it? Is it in your logs?\n• **Cost**: can usage (or an attacker) run up a bill? Is there a cap or alert?\n• **Failure**: what happens when the database is down, the disk is full, or a dependency breaks? Is there a backup?',
    how: 'Run it as a checklist, with evidence, like any audit:\n1. **Secrets**: search the code and history for `key`, `secret`, `token`, `password`. Anything in front-end code is public (anyone can open devtools and read it). Secrets belong in environment variables on the server, and the file holding them (`.env`) belongs in `.gitignore`. If a secret was ever committed, treat it as leaked: **rotate it** (make a new one, revoke the old one).\n2. **Validation**: for every route that accepts input, confirm the server checks type, presence and range, and builds saved records from known fields only. Browser checks are a convenience, not protection.\n3. **Injection**: any SQL built by gluing strings together with user input is an injection hole. Use parameterized queries (`?` placeholders) every time.\n4. **Privacy**: list every personal field you store. Delete the ones you don\'t need. Never log passwords, tokens or full personal details. Don\'t show private data (emails, exact locations) on public pages.\n5. **Cost**: list every paid or metered service. Set spending caps and alerts. Add rate limits to anything an anonymous visitor can trigger.\n6. **Failure and recovery**: what is the backup? Has a restore ever been tried? What does the user see when something fails?\n7. **Dependencies and licences**: are packages from trusted sources, kept up to date, and is their licence compatible with what you are doing?\n8. **Write it down**: a short list of findings, each with severity (critical / high / medium / low) and the fix. Fix criticals before launch, no exceptions.',
    usage: [
      { code: 'SELF-AUDIT: Summit Log v1\n[CRITICAL] Map API key is in the React code → move the call to the server, rotate the key\n[CRITICAL] /api/climbs?peak= builds SQL by string joining → parameterized query\n[HIGH]     POST saves req.body as-is → validate + pick known fields\n[HIGH]     .env not in .gitignore → add it; rotate the database password\n[MEDIUM]   Logs print signup emails → log ids only\n[LOW]      No spending alert on the host → set one at a small amount', note: 'Findings with severity and a concrete fix each. Criticals block launch.' },
      { code: '// BEFORE: trusts the client completely\napp.post("/api/climbs", (req, res) => { climbs.push(req.body); res.json(req.body) })\n\n// AFTER: validate, then save only known fields, with a server-owned id\napp.post("/api/climbs", (req, res) => {\n  const errors = validateClimb(req.body)\n  if (errors.length) return res.status(400).json({ errors })\n  const { peak, heightM, summited, date } = req.body\n  const climb = { id: nextId++, peak: peak.trim(), heightM, summited, date }\n  climbs.push(climb)\n  res.status(201).json(climb)\n})', note: 'A typical self-audit fix: from "trust everything" to "check everything".' },
    ],
    uses: ['Before any launch, demo or job application link.', 'After adding anything that touches personal data, money, logins or paid services.', 'As a regular habit (say, once a month) on anything that stays online.', 'In interviews: walking through how you audited your own project shows real-world judgement.'],
    when: ['Before the app goes public, every time.', 'Whenever you add a new data field, a new third-party service or a new way for users to send input.', 'Immediately if you suspect a secret leaked: rotate first, investigate second.'],
    whenNot: ['Don\'t treat it as optional because the project is "small" or "just for me". Small apps with leaked keys still get abused and still run up bills.', 'Don\'t audit only the code. Check the repo history, the logs, the hosting settings and the billing page too.', 'Don\'t "fix" a leaked secret by deleting it from the latest commit. It is still in the history; rotate it.'],
    mistakes: [
      { prose: 'Hiding an API key in the front end by giving it a boring variable name or splitting it into pieces.', why: 'Everything sent to the browser is readable in devtools. If the browser can use it, anyone can copy it. Move the call to the server.' },
      { prose: 'Adding `.env` to `.gitignore` after it was already committed and pushed.', why: 'gitignore only stops future tracking. The secret is in the history and on the remote. Rotate every value in it.' },
      { prose: 'Logging whole request bodies "for debugging", including passwords and emails.', why: 'Logs are copied, shared and kept for a long time. Sensitive data in logs is a privacy leak waiting to happen.' },
    ],
    tips: ['Audit as if a stranger wrote the code. You will catch far more than when you audit "your" code.', 'The cheapest protection is collecting less: data you never stored can never leak.', 'Set a spending alert on every paid service the day you sign up, before you write a line of code against it.'],
    related: ['final-testing', 'red-flags', 'permissions', 'gitignore', 'launch-checklist'],
  },

  // ─────────────── Off the app: shipping and showing ───────────────
  'launch-checklist': {
    term: 'Launch checklist (from your computer to the internet)', short: 'The real-computer steps to create, version, push and deploy your app, in order.',
    what: 'Everything in this game runs in practice engines. The real finish is doing it for real: a project folder on your own computer, tracked in Git, pushed to a remote repository, and deployed to a hosting service so anyone with the link can use it.\n\nThe launch checklist is the ordered list of those **off-app steps**, from an empty folder to a live URL. Experienced developers still use a checklist. Climbers do a buddy check before every climb for the same reason: a routine check prevents the big mistake.',
    how: 'The steps (exact install commands for your system are in **💻 On your system** below):\n1. **Tools**: install Node.js (which brings npm) and Git. Check with `node --version`, `npm --version`, `git --version`.\n2. **Create the project**: `mkdir summit-log`, `cd summit-log`, `npm init -y` (writes `package.json`), `npm install express`.\n3. **Protect it**: create `.gitignore` with `node_modules` and `.env` on their own lines, BEFORE the first commit.\n4. **Write the server** (`index.js`) and read the port from the environment: `const port = process.env.PORT || 3000`. Hosting services tell your app which port to use through `PORT`.\n5. **Add scripts** to `package.json`: `"start": "node index.js"`, and a `"test"` script. Hosts typically run `npm start`.\n6. **Run it locally**: `npm start`, then open `http://localhost:3000/api/health` in your browser. Stop it with **Ctrl+C**. To use a `.env` file locally, start with `node --env-file=.env index.js` (Node 20.6 or newer); Node does not read `.env` by itself.\n7. **Front end** (if separate): `npm create vite@latest summit-log-ui -- --template react`, then `cd summit-log-ui`, `npm install`, `npm run dev`. `npm run build` makes the static files you deploy.\n8. **Version it**: `git init`, `git add .`, `git status` (check that `node_modules` and `.env` are NOT listed), `git commit -m "Walking skeleton"`.\n9. **Push it**: create an empty repository on your Git host, then `git remote add origin <url>`, `git branch -M main`, `git push -u origin main`.\n10. **Deploy**: on a hosting service, create a new project from your repository. Set the start command (`npm start`) and add your secrets as **environment variables in the host\'s settings** (never in the repo). Every push to `main` can then redeploy automatically.\n11. **Check live**: open the live URL, run your manual test checklist on a real phone, and watch the host\'s logs for errors.\n12. **Set a spending alert** on the host and every paid service.',
    usage: [
      { code: '$ mkdir summit-log\n$ cd summit-log\n$ npm init -y\n$ npm install express\n$ echo "node_modules" > .gitignore\n$ echo ".env" >> .gitignore', note: 'Steps 2 and 3. The second echo uses >> to ADD a line instead of replacing the file.' },
      { code: 'const express = require("express")\nconst app = express()\napp.use(express.json())\napp.get("/api/health", (req, res) => res.json({ ok: true }))\n\nconst port = process.env.PORT || 3000\napp.listen(port, () => console.log("Summit Log API on port " + port))', out: 'Summit Log API on port 3000', note: 'index.js, the walking skeleton. On a host, PORT is set for you; locally it falls back to 3000.' },
      { code: '$ git init\n$ git add .\n$ git status\n$ git commit -m "Walking skeleton: health check"\n$ git remote add origin https://github.com/YOUR-NAME/summit-log.git\n$ git branch -M main\n$ git push -u origin main', note: 'Steps 8 and 9. Check git status before committing: node_modules and .env must not appear.' },
    ],
    uses: ['Putting any Node project online: portfolio apps, tools for friends, client work.', 'Repeating the same safe routine for every future project.', 'Handing a project to someone else: the checklist doubles as setup instructions in your README.'],
    when: ['When the walking skeleton works: deploy early, so deploy problems surface while the app is tiny.', 'For every new project you start on a real computer.', 'Before every release: run the end of the list (tests, status check, live check) again.'],
    whenNot: ['Don\'t commit before `.gitignore` exists. Pushing `node_modules` is just messy; pushing `.env` leaks your secrets.', 'Don\'t put secrets in the code "just to test the deploy". Use the host\'s environment-variable settings from the start.', 'Don\'t deploy something that handles real personal data or payments until its self-audit has no critical or high findings.', 'Don\'t hard-code `app.listen(3000)` for a hosted app. Many hosts assign the port; read `process.env.PORT`.'],
    mistakes: [
      { wrong: 'git add .   (with no .gitignore yet)', result: 'Hundreds of node_modules files staged, and .env with your passwords about to be committed', why: 'Create .gitignore first. If it already happened, and nothing is committed yet, unstage with `git rm -r --cached .`, add the .gitignore, and add again.' },
      { wrong: 'app.listen(3000)   (on a host)', result: 'The deploy "succeeds", but the site never answers', why: 'The host expected your app on the port in process.env.PORT. Use `process.env.PORT || 3000`.' },
      { wrong: 'npm start   (no "start" script in package.json)', result: 'npm error Missing script: "start"', why: 'Add `"start": "node index.js"` to the scripts section. Hosts commonly run npm start.' },
      { wrong: 'git push -u origin main', result: "error: src refspec main does not match any", why: 'There is no commit on a branch called main yet. Commit first, and run `git branch -M main` to name the branch.' },
    ],
    tips: ['Deploy the walking skeleton in week one. Then deploy problems show up while the app is still tiny.', 'Read the host\'s build and runtime logs. When a deploy fails, the answer is almost always in there.', 'Keep the exact setup commands in your README under "Run it yourself". You will need them again later.'],
    related: ['npm', 'package-json', 'gitignore', 'push-pull', 'self-audit', 'presenting-work'],
    os: {
      ubuntu: 'Install Git with `sudo apt install git`. For a current Node.js, the distro package (`sudo apt install nodejs npm`) is often old; a version manager such as **nvm** (install it from its official page, then `nvm install --lts`) gives you the current LTS. Open a terminal with **Ctrl+Alt+T**; stop a running server with **Ctrl+C**.',
      fedora: 'Install with `sudo dnf install nodejs git` (Fedora\'s Node packages are fairly current). Check with `node --version`.',
      arch: 'Install with `sudo pacman -S nodejs npm git`.',
      linux: 'Install Git and Node.js with your package manager, or use **nvm** for Node to get the current LTS version. Stop a running server with **Ctrl+C**.',
      mac: 'With Homebrew: `brew install node git`. (Typing `git` the first time may also offer to install Apple\'s command line tools, which include Git.) Open Terminal with **Cmd+Space** → "Terminal". Stop a running server with **Ctrl+C** (Ctrl, not Cmd).',
      windows: 'Work inside **WSL** (Ubuntu), so every command in this course works as written: open the Ubuntu app, then `sudo apt install git`, and install Node with **nvm** (install nvm from its official page, then `nvm install --lts`). Keep your projects in your Linux home folder (`~/summit-log`), not under `/mnt/c`, for speed. Open `http://localhost:3000` in your normal browser; WSL forwards it.',
      chromeos: 'Turn on the Linux development environment, open its Terminal, then `sudo apt install git`, and install Node with **nvm** (install nvm from its official page, then `nvm install --lts`). `http://localhost:3000` opens in Chrome.',
      phone: 'A phone can\'t run this setup itself. You can still do a lot: create the repository on your Git host\'s website, watch deploy logs in the host\'s dashboard, and run your manual test checklist on the live site. With an SSH app you can also drive a computer or cloud server from your phone.',
    },
  },
  'presenting-work': {
    term: 'Presenting your work', short: 'Explaining what you built, how it works, and what you decided, so others can understand, use and trust it.',
    what: 'People can only use an app they understand. Presenting your work means packaging it so a stranger (a user, a teammate, an interviewer) can get it in minutes: **what** it does, **why** it exists, **how** it works, **how to run it**, and **what you would do next**.\n\nIt has three common forms:\n• **The README**: the front page of your repository.\n• **The demo**: a two-minute live walkthrough, or a short screen recording.\n• **The explanation**: telling the story of one request from click to database and back, and the decisions you made on the way.\n\nA good README and demo show that the work exists and teach others how to use it.',
    how: 'A strong README, in this order:\n1. **Name and one-line pitch**: "Summit Log: log every climb and see your summit stats."\n2. **Live link and a screenshot** (or short GIF) near the top.\n3. **Features**: the requirements you shipped.\n4. **How it works**: the stack (React → Express → SQL database) and the request flow in a few lines; the API contract table.\n5. **Run it yourself**: the exact commands (`npm install`, `npm start`, `npm test`) and the environment variables needed (names only, never values).\n6. **Decisions and trade-offs**: why this database, what you cut from the MVP and why.\n7. **Security and privacy notes**: what you audited and fixed.\n8. **What\'s next**: the Later list.\n\nA two-minute demo: **problem** (15 s) → **show the main path** working (60 s) → **one interesting thing under the hood** (30 s) → **what you learned or would do next** (15 s). Rehearse it once. Show the real thing working; don\'t read slides.\n\nWhen explaining technical work, go **end to end** (click → request → route → validation → database → response → screen), use the real names (`POST /api/climbs`, `201`), and be honest about limits. "I haven\'t handled two people editing the same climb yet" builds more trust than pretending it is perfect.',
    usage: [
      { code: '# Summit Log\nLog every climb and see your summit stats.\n\n**Live:** https://summit-log.example.app\n\n## Features\n- Log a climb (peak, height, date, summited?)\n- Filter to summited climbs · delete mistakes · stats\n\n## How it works\nReact screen → Express API (/api/climbs) → SQL database.\nInputs are validated on the server; queries are parameterized.\n\n## Run it yourself\nnpm install\nnpm test\nnpm start   # needs DATABASE_URL set (see .env.example)\n\n## Next\nPhotos, offline mode.', note: 'A README that answers what, how, and how to run it, in one screen.' },
      { code: '"When you tap Log climb, React sends POST /api/climbs with the climb as JSON.\n Express validates it; a bad height gets a 400 with a clear message.\n A parameterized INSERT saves it, the server replies 201 with the saved climb,\n and React adds it to state, so the list re-renders. Here it is live."', note: 'Explaining the request flow end to end, in your own words: the radio check in this camp trains exactly this.' },
    ],
    uses: ['Portfolio projects and job applications: the README is often the first (and only) thing a reviewer reads.', 'Open-source projects: a clear README is how strangers start using and contributing.', 'Handing work to a teammate or client.', 'Interviews: "walk me through something you built" is one of the most common questions there is.'],
    when: ['When the MVP works, before sharing the link anywhere.', 'Update it with every release: features, setup steps, screenshots.', 'Before any interview: rehearse the two-minute demo and the end-to-end explanation.'],
    whenNot: ['Don\'t put secrets, real passwords or personal data in the README, screenshots or demo recordings. Use sample data.', 'Don\'t oversell. Claiming "secure, scalable, production-ready" invites exactly the checks you learned to run, and they will find the gaps.', 'Don\'t write a novel. A visitor decides in about thirty seconds whether to keep reading.'],
    mistakes: [
      { prose: 'A README that is just the default template, or one line: "my app".', why: 'Nobody can tell what it does or how to run it, so nobody will. Lead with the pitch, the link and the run commands.' },
      { prose: 'A demo that starts with fifteen minutes of setup and slides.', why: 'People remember the moment it worked. Open on the running app and show the main path first.' },
      { prose: 'A screenshot that shows a real API key in the browser devtools or a terminal.', why: 'Screenshots are as public as code. Crop or blur, or better, demo with sample keys and data.' },
    ],
    tips: ['Ask a friend to follow your "Run it yourself" steps on their machine without help. Every place they get stuck is a README fix.', 'Keep a short decisions log while you build. It turns into the best part of your README and your interview answers.', 'Say what you would do next. It shows you understand the limits of what you built.'],
    related: ['project-planning', 'launch-checklist', 'self-audit', 'api-contract'],
    os: {
      linux: 'For a demo recording, many desktops have a built-in screen recorder (on GNOME: **Ctrl+Shift+Alt+R** starts and stops it); other options are in your software centre.',
      mac: 'Press **Cmd+Shift+5** to record the screen or a window, or take a screenshot of part of the screen.',
      windows: 'Press **Win+Shift+S** for a screenshot of part of the screen; on Windows 11 the Snipping Tool can also record a short screen video.',
      chromeos: 'Press **Ctrl+Shift+Show windows** to open the screen capture tool, which can also record video.',
      phone: 'Use your phone\'s built-in screen recording (in the quick settings or control centre) to film your live app for a demo.',
    },
  },
}
